# .mutation-allowlist - surviving (LIVED) mutants accepted as already known.
#
# SCOPE: this allowlist covers ONLY the packages inside the mutation gate scope
# (see MUTATE_EXCLUDE in the Makefile): internal/cli, internal/db, internal/model
# and internal/config. Mutations outside that scope are not run at all, so they
# can never appear here. internal/tui and cmd/ are still excluded because they
# have no tests (uncovered code produces no mutants, so gating them would be a
# green check that cannot fail).
#
# Format:  <MUTATOR> <file>:<line>
#
# Add an entry ONLY when the mutant is provably equivalent (no test can ever kill
# it, because the mutation does not change observable behaviour) or the gap is a
# conscious, accepted risk. Adding a real test gap here hides a real risk, and
# mutation testing exists precisely to surface those.
#
# Entries are keyed by LINE NUMBER, so inserting code above an entry shifts it
# and will surface a new red on purpose: re-verify the gap and update this file,
# do not regenerate it wholesale (that would silently re-hide real gaps).
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Both remaining entries are the SAME expression, `x > epsilon` / `x <= epsilon`
# where epsilon is 1e-9, and both need a float to land EXACTLY on epsilon. That
# is unreachable, and the proof is arithmetic, not a guess:
#
#   capacity is only ever 1.0 or `1.0 - consume`, and remaining is only ever an
#   input estimate minus such a value. Every subtraction there is exact
#   (Sterbenz: the operands are within a factor of 2), so the result is always a
#   multiple of 2^-53. The float64 nearest 1e-9 is 332103651088021 * 2^-82 with
#   an ODD mantissa, so it is not a multiple of 2^-53 and can never be produced.
#   Verified at runtime, with variables so the compiler cannot fold the
#   arithmetic at compile time: 1.0-(1.0-1e-9) = 9.999999717180685e-10 != 1e-9.
#
# The equivalent way to remove them is to stop doing day arithmetic in floats
# (integer minutes), which is a behaviour-visible refactor of the scheduler, not
# a test change. Until that happens these two are documented, not hidden.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# `if capacity <= epsilon` -> `capacity < epsilon`. Differs only at capacity ==
# epsilon, which the arithmetic above rules out.
CONDITIONALS_BOUNDARY internal/model/gantt.go:153

# `if remaining > epsilon` -> `remaining >= epsilon`. Same threshold, same
# unreachability. Note the loop guard on the line above uses the same
# comparison and IS killable, because `remaining` there is the raw input
# estimate: TestBuildScheduleEstimateEnElSuelo passes epsilon exactly.
CONDITIONALS_BOUNDARY internal/model/gantt.go:167

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# internal/cli
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# `if n, err := strconv.Atoi(v); err == nil && n > 0` -> `n >= 0`. The only
# differing input is `--weeks 0`, and both forms end up with weeks == 0, which
# the caller immediately replaces with the config value (`if weeks <= 0`).
# There is no input for which the two branches lead anywhere different.
CONDITIONALS_BOUNDARY internal/cli/cli.go:828

# `if len(updates) > 0` / `len(tagsAdd) > 0` / `len(tagsRemove) > 0` -> `>= 0`.
# All three guard an empty collection. Calling them with an empty one is a
# no-op: AddTaskTags/RemoveTaskTags with no tags change nothing, and
# UpdateTask with no fields builds "SET updated_at = ?" -- the only difference
# is that a timestamp moves, which is what the guard exists to prevent and what
# no behaviour test pins. TestUpdateWithoutFlagsIsAnExactNoop covers the
# fields and the tags surviving; it cannot catch a sub-second timestamp bump.
CONDITIONALS_BOUNDARY internal/cli/cli.go:1056
CONDITIONALS_BOUNDARY internal/cli/cli.go:1066
CONDITIONALS_BOUNDARY internal/cli/cli.go:1071
